1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,468
Look at these four numbers: point two, point four, point six, point one.

2
00:00:05,468 --> 00:00:10,328
Ignore their exact sizes and rank them from smallest to largest.

3
00:00:10,328 --> 00:00:15,264
In their original positions, the ranks are two, three, four, one.

4
00:00:15,633 --> 00:00:21,216
That is an order pattern. Two players choose different patterns, then watch the same

5
00:00:21,216 --> 00:00:26,068
stream of random numbers. After each new number, inspect the latest four.

6
00:00:26,068 --> 00:00:28,593
Whoever sees their pattern first wins.

7
00:00:28,967 --> 00:00:32,510
Suppose the first player chooses two, three, four, one.

8
00:00:32,510 --> 00:00:37,986
Move its final symbol to the front. You get one, two, three, four: an increasing run.

9
00:00:37,986 --> 00:00:43,397
This right rotation is a winning reply, even though both patterns are equally likely

10
00:00:43,397 --> 00:00:44,879
in one isolated window.

11
00:00:45,267 --> 00:00:51,420
Here is a concrete stream. The first four values increase, so the rotation wins immediately.

12
00:00:51,420 --> 00:00:56,235
If we continued, the next window would have order two, three, four, one.

13
00:00:56,235 --> 00:00:59,379
The original pattern arrives one draw too late.

14
00:00:59,767 --> 00:01:06,173
The theorem applies to independent observations drawn from one common continuous distribution.

15
00:01:06,173 --> 00:01:10,398
Independence means previous draws do not affect the next draw.

16
00:01:10,398 --> 00:01:13,602
Continuity rules out ties with probability one.

17
00:01:13,602 --> 00:01:17,623
We compare relative order, not particular numerical values.

18
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,625
Four distinct independent draws have twenty-four equally likely orders.

19
00:01:22,625 --> 00:01:27,576
Each chosen pattern has probability one over twenty-four in a single window.

20
00:01:27,576 --> 00:01:30,833
But neighbouring windows share three observations.

21
00:01:30,833 --> 00:01:34,416
Equal snapshot probabilities do not make the race fair.

22
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:37,744
Let us examine that shared information.

23
00:01:37,744 --> 00:01:41,444
Call our five observations x zero through x four.

24
00:01:41,444 --> 00:01:47,710
An increasing window first requires x zero below x one, below x two, below x three.

25
00:01:47,710 --> 00:01:53,448
The next window must place x four below x one to match the original pattern.

26
00:01:53,833 --> 00:01:58,734
So x zero and x four both lie below the same chain of three values.

27
00:01:58,734 --> 00:02:02,610
Only their order relative to each other is undecided.

28
00:02:02,610 --> 00:02:05,975
There are exactly two compatible total orders.

29
00:02:05,975 --> 00:02:09,193
Watch the two bottom values exchange places.

30
00:02:09,567 --> 00:02:14,913
Two increasing windows in a row have only one compatible order: all five values must

31
00:02:14,913 --> 00:02:20,323
increase. Starting with the original pattern cannot immediately produce either chosen

32
00:02:20,323 --> 00:02:25,479
pattern here, because the shared comparisons would have to rise and fall at once.

33
00:02:25,867 --> 00:02:31,516
There are five factorial, or one hundred and twenty, equally likely orders of five draws.

34
00:02:31,516 --> 00:02:36,912
The signed overlap comparison gives two minus one, divided by one hundred and twenty.

35
00:02:36,912 --> 00:02:42,244
This is one local contribution. It is not the final winning probability or the final

36
00:02:42,244 --> 00:02:43,387
winning advantage.

37
00:02:43,767 --> 00:02:49,320
For every pattern of length k, the corresponding one-position overlap contributes at

38
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:54,940
least one over k plus one factorial. That gives the proof a positive starting amount.

39
00:02:54,940 --> 00:03:00,692
But all longer overlaps still need to be included before we can conclude anything about

40
00:03:00,692 --> 00:03:01,287
the race.

41
00:03:01,667 --> 00:03:05,481
Why are some contributions added and others subtracted?

42
00:03:05,481 --> 00:03:11,584
Start with a counting example. Six objects have one property and five have another, with

43
00:03:11,584 --> 00:03:17,410
two objects in both groups. Six plus five counts those two twice, so subtract two to

44
00:03:17,410 --> 00:03:19,283
get nine different objects.

45
00:03:19,667 --> 00:03:25,810
This is inclusion and exclusion. With more events, subtract double overlaps and add triple

46
00:03:25,810 --> 00:03:31,613
overlaps back. For the race, mark possible occurrences along the stream and alternate

47
00:03:31,613 --> 00:03:36,323
signs. A chain of overlapping marked occurrences is called a cluster.

48
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:39,594
Here are three consecutive occurrence starts.

49
00:03:39,594 --> 00:03:43,645
A cluster can mark only the first and last, giving a plus sign.

50
00:03:43,645 --> 00:03:46,603
Or it can mark all three, giving a minus sign.

51
00:03:46,603 --> 00:03:51,748
For a sequence satisfying both descriptions, those contributions cancel exactly.

52
00:03:52,133 --> 00:03:55,384
The paper proves a recurrence for this cancellation.

53
00:03:55,384 --> 00:04:00,886
For each new occurrence, add the earlier surviving coefficients less than k starts away,

54
00:04:00,886 --> 00:04:06,199
then change the sign. Starting at minus one, the only possible coefficients are minus

55
00:04:06,199 --> 00:04:07,637
one, zero and plus one.

56
00:04:08,033 --> 00:04:13,310
For length four with an occurrence at every start, the coefficients begin minus one,

57
00:04:13,310 --> 00:04:15,948
plus one, zero, zero, minus one, plus one.

58
00:04:15,948 --> 00:04:21,350
Nonzero signs alternate. In any four consecutive start positions, at most two survive.

59
00:04:21,350 --> 00:04:25,433
This prevents the number of markings from overwhelming the bound.

60
00:04:25,833 --> 00:04:29,396
There is another useful counting trick for short overlaps.

61
00:04:29,396 --> 00:04:32,282
Shared observations already have a fixed order.

62
00:04:32,282 --> 00:04:35,906
Between them, private observations form two ordered chains.

63
00:04:35,906 --> 00:04:40,881
Combining those chains means choosing how their blue and teal entries interleave.

64
00:04:41,267 --> 00:04:45,072
With two entries of each colour, there are six interleavings.

65
00:04:45,072 --> 00:04:48,939
For a simple illustration, require the first entry to be blue.

66
00:04:48,939 --> 00:04:54,304
Three choices remain. Swapping blue and teal sends each allowed choice to a different,

67
00:04:54,304 --> 00:04:55,427
disallowed choice.

68
00:04:55,800 --> 00:05:01,493
In the proof, differing private ranks impose unequal colour counts at a boundary between

69
00:05:01,493 --> 00:05:05,504
shared values. Swapping colours again produces a disjoint set.

70
00:05:05,504 --> 00:05:09,257
So at most half of all balanced interleavings are allowed.

71
00:05:09,257 --> 00:05:13,656
This bounds the short overlaps that could work against the rotation.

72
00:05:14,033 --> 00:05:16,420
What about arbitrarily distant overlaps?

73
00:05:16,420 --> 00:05:21,851
Group actual occurrences into a clump whenever consecutive starts are less than k positions

74
00:05:21,851 --> 00:05:25,789
apart. Focus on the clump containing the occurrence at start zero.

75
00:05:25,789 --> 00:05:29,489
We need a bound on how far this particular clump can continue.

76
00:05:29,867 --> 00:05:35,377
For length four, finish observing one anchor window, then look for another occurrence

77
00:05:35,377 --> 00:05:37,905
starting at least four positions later.

78
00:05:37,905 --> 00:05:43,674
To remain in the same clump, the next anchor must start within three candidate positions.

79
00:05:43,674 --> 00:05:48,731
Those candidate windows use observations fresh after the previous anchor ends.

80
00:05:49,100 --> 00:05:53,931
Each candidate window has probability two over twenty-four of matching either pattern.

81
00:05:53,931 --> 00:05:58,537
Add the three probabilities to bound the chance of any match: at most one quarter.

82
00:05:58,537 --> 00:06:00,840
The candidate windows overlap each other.

83
00:06:00,840 --> 00:06:04,604
This is a union bound, not an assumption that they are independent.

84
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:10,130
In general, r is twice the quantity k minus one, divided by k factorial.

85
00:06:10,130 --> 00:06:13,835
Repeating the necessary condition gives powers of r.

86
00:06:13,835 --> 00:06:18,252
One quarter, one sixteenth, one sixty-four: these shrink fast.

87
00:06:18,252 --> 00:06:23,168
Adding the geometric series bounds everything beyond a chosen cutoff.

88
00:06:23,567 --> 00:06:27,234
Only anchors that remain in the original clump are counted.

89
00:06:27,234 --> 00:06:31,771
New occurrences elsewhere in the endless stream do not extend that clump.

90
00:06:31,771 --> 00:06:37,178
Combined with the cancellation bound, this gives a rigorous limit on the entire omitted

91
00:06:37,178 --> 00:06:40,535
contribution, not just a guess from a long simulation.

92
00:06:40,933 --> 00:06:43,612
The proof now separates pattern lengths.

93
00:06:43,612 --> 00:06:46,694
Three was already handled by Elizalde and Lin.

94
00:06:46,694 --> 00:06:52,186
Lengths four through seven use exact finite calculations with a proven tail bound.

95
00:06:52,186 --> 00:06:56,941
Every length at least eight is covered by one uniform written argument.

96
00:06:57,333 --> 00:07:03,079
For the infinite range, scale the signed comparison so the initial positive contribution

97
00:07:03,079 --> 00:07:08,433
is at least one. Subtract bounds for all adverse short overlaps and the long tail.

98
00:07:08,433 --> 00:07:14,113
The remaining guaranteed margin is positive, about zero point zero nine five, for every

99
00:07:14,113 --> 00:07:15,549
length at least eight.

100
00:07:15,933 --> 00:07:21,995
This margin is a scaled signed comparison, not a universal nine-point-five percent winning

101
00:07:21,995 --> 00:07:27,989
advantage. A proved first-occurrence identity turns its positive sign into the conclusion

102
00:07:27,989 --> 00:07:31,222
that the rotation wins more than half the races.

103
00:07:31,222 --> 00:07:35,397
The conversion factor is positive but depends on the patterns.

104
00:07:35,767 --> 00:07:41,329
At the shorter lengths, count all compatible total orders up to a specified span, using

105
00:07:41,329 --> 00:07:46,828
integers. Then compare the exact partial sum with a bound on the whole remaining tail.

106
00:07:46,828 --> 00:07:52,135
If the positive partial sum is larger, no unseen contribution can reverse its sign.

107
00:07:52,533 --> 00:07:58,079
For length four, the scaled partial sum is greater than three quarters, while the absolute

108
00:07:58,079 --> 00:08:01,653
tail is at most five twelfths. The gap is safely positive.

109
00:08:01,653 --> 00:08:04,118
The other three rows pass the same test.

110
00:08:04,118 --> 00:08:08,493
These are exact rational comparisons, not rounded simulation estimates.

111
00:08:08,867 --> 00:08:13,771
There are two thousand, nine hundred and fifty-two representatives across lengths four

112
00:08:13,771 --> 00:08:18,904
through seven. Reflecting all ranks covers the other half of the patterns without changing

113
00:08:18,904 --> 00:08:23,866
the game. Python and C plus plus independently reconstructed every integer coefficient,

114
00:08:23,866 --> 00:08:25,235
and their outputs agree.

115
00:08:25,633 --> 00:08:30,555
For length three, an extra check counts races through eighteen observations.

116
00:08:30,555 --> 00:08:36,060
Give every unresolved race to the original pattern, the worst possible assumption for

117
00:08:36,060 --> 00:08:41,564
the rotation. Even then, the original pattern wins with probability below forty-seven

118
00:08:41,564 --> 00:08:45,385
percent. This verifies a known case, with no novelty claim.

119
00:08:45,767 --> 00:08:51,033
Put the ranges together. For every length at least three, and every starting permutation,

120
00:08:51,033 --> 00:08:56,122
moving the last symbol to the front produces a reply with winning probability strictly

121
00:08:56,122 --> 00:09:01,271
above one half. The result is specific to the independent continuous observation model.

122
00:09:01,667 --> 00:09:06,093
Winning does not mean best. Another reply might have a larger advantage.

123
00:09:06,093 --> 00:09:11,442
This work does not find the optimal reply for every pattern or give every exact winning

124
00:09:11,442 --> 00:09:16,115
probability. The cluster method itself is prior work, credited in the paper.

125
00:09:16,500 --> 00:09:22,067
Remember the mechanism: equal snapshots can have unequal arrival times, overlapping windows

126
00:09:22,067 --> 00:09:27,268
create a directional advantage, and cancellation plus tail bounds controls everything

127
00:09:27,268 --> 00:09:31,000
the small example leaves out. Correctness confidence is high.

128
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:34,548
Originality confidence is moderate after a bounded search.

129
00:09:34,933 --> 00:09:41,393
The paper, exact certificates and independent execution records accompany this video.

130
00:09:41,393 --> 00:09:45,801
The argument is computer assisted, not formalised in Lean.

131
00:09:45,801 --> 00:09:49,981
Valyu Research, 2026. Narration uses a synthetic voice.
